  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x(1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}\u2061x$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x(1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x({sec}^{2}\u2061x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{cos}^{2}\u2061x(1+\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x)}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x(1+\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
